# Stakeholder Register — Initiating
#
# Process Groups: A Practice Guide, 13.1 Identify Stakeholders. Engagement levels come from
# the Stakeholder Engagement Assessment Matrix used in 13.2.

screen: stakeholders
title: Stakeholder Register
phase: Initiating
process: 13.1 Identify Stakeholders
order: 20
requires: charter
teaches: |
  How to find the people who can affect the project or be affected by it, and
  what to do about the gap between how engaged someone is and how engaged the
  project needs them to be. By the end you should be able to explain why power
  and interest are tracked separately.
before: |
  The charter, because its key stakeholder list is what you expand here. If
  you do not know who is sponsoring the work, you are not ready to plan how to
  manage them.
after: |
  How you communicate for the rest of the project. Every stakeholder whose
  current engagement is below what the project needs is a piece of work you now
  own.
intro: |
  The stakeholder register lists everyone who can affect the project or is
  affected by it, records how much each one matters, and compares how engaged
  they are now with how engaged they need to be.

  The gap between current and desired engagement is the work.
source: |
  Process Groups: A Practice Guide (PMI, 2022), process 13.1 Identify Stakeholders, in the Initiating
  process group. The engagement levels come from the Stakeholder Engagement
  Assessment Matrix used in 13.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement.

field register
  prompt: Who are the people around this project, and how engaged does each one need to be?
  check rows>=2: A project with one stakeholder is a hobby. Include anyone who can affect the outcome, not only the people asking for it.
  check column:role: Every stakeholder needs a role in the project. 'Interested' is not a role.
  label: Stakeholders
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: matrix
  title_column: name
  rows_column: power
  cols_column: interest
  tone_column: current
  source: |
    Stakeholder register contents: identification information, assessment
    information and classification.
  why: |
    You cannot manage expectations you have not written down. Power and
    interest decide how much attention each person gets; the gap between
    current and desired engagement is what you actually have to do about it.

    The classic grid: high power + high interest means manage closely; high
    power + low interest means keep satisfied; low power + high interest means
    keep informed; low power + low interest means monitor.
  if_blank: |
    The person with the most power over the outcome is the one you forgot to
    ask.
  example: |
    the reviewer | Final reviewer | Says whether it is right | High | High | Neutral | Supportive
  column name
    label: Name
    type: line
    width: 2
  column role
    label: Role in project
    type: line
    width: 2
  column expectations
    label: Expectations / requirements
    type: text
    width: 4
  column power
    label: Power
    type: choice
    options: High=accent, Low=faint
    width: 1
  column interest
    label: Interest
    type: choice
    options: High=accent, Low=faint
    width: 1
  column current
    label: Current engagement
    type: choice
    options: Unaware=faint, Resistant=danger, Neutral=neutral, Supportive=ok, Leading=accent
    width: 2
  column desired
    label: Desired engagement
    type: choice
    options: Unaware=faint, Resistant=danger, Neutral=neutral, Supportive=ok, Leading=accent
    width: 2

field strategy
  prompt: For anyone not as engaged as the project needs, what will you actually do about it?
  check filled: Without this the register is a list of names. The gap between current and desired engagement is the work.
  label: Engagement notes
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    13.2 Plan Stakeholder Engagement: strategies to move stakeholders from
    their current engagement to the level the project needs.
  why: |
    For every stakeholder whose current engagement is below the desired level,
    this says what you will do about it. A register without this is a list of
    names.
  if_blank: |
    The gaps in the table stay gaps.
  example: |
    Give the final reviewer the charter up front, so "right" is judged against
    written criteria rather than discovered at the review.
